{"kind":"assert","text":"Hugh: isolate the documentation citation gate from the active workroom's moving verification witness.\n\nThis is an efficiency recommendation from repeated local validation failures, not a new red GitHub CI finding. Current CI and Security passed at 32660749aad93bff40c93f6aedf6b80a186dd489. Across the recent oversized-attachment, S1, npm-advisory and record-guide deliveries, authors recorded the same gate refusing a shorter verified frontier after another session advanced the shared witness. The npm review I performed preserved the actual limit: the all-module race run failed only this gate at depths22850/22851, then the whole docset race retry passed in153.456s. That is passing coverage across two runs, not an uninterrupted green run. Other examples include22331/22332,22449/22450 and the record-guide run at22900. The [selected durable reports](reported-gate-failures.json) retain their full wording and test limits. These reports establish recurring rework; they do not measure average lost time or prove every reported failure has one cause.\n\nThe [citation gate](internal/docset/anchor_test.go@32660749aad93bff40c93f6aedf6b80a186dd489:40) opens the actual development workroom and calls Snapshot. The [snapshot path](internal/app/app.go@32660749aad93bff40c93f6aedf6b80a186dd489:2749) then records its verification against the newest stored witness. The [transaction rule](internal/app/app.go@32660749aad93bff40c93f6aedf6b80a186dd489:2887) deliberately refuses a result behind that witness, and the [rollback check](internal/app/attach.go@32660749aad93bff40c93f6aedf6b80a186dd489:104) expresses the security condition. A concurrent genuine extension can therefore invalidate a captured test read. Weakening that rule, accepting an old snapshot in the active reader, or resetting the witness would address the wrong boundary.\n\nRecommendation: make this one citation test use an isolated, stable view of the real signed workroom captured for the run, while preserving the page source under test. Start with a small feasibility check for the existing capture/verification interfaces; choose the least copying and configuration machinery that can bind the exact frontier and interpreter. Do not copy actor secrets, mutate the shared witness, replace a resident, or invent an unsigned projection fixture. Record which frontier was checked. Keep a separately explicit live audit if needed; a source test result must not imply freshness forever.\n\nSuccess means the test still resolves real signed acts and still fails for a fabricated citation or an unusable retired basis. Its documented no-workroom skip remains explicit, not a fallback for arbitrary verification failures. A deterministic control should advance the real room while the isolated citation check runs and show that genuine concurrent progress neither changes the checked frontier nor causes an unrelated test failure. Existing production controls must continue refusing actual rollback, forks and invalid verification. The implementation review should judge whether this is achievable inside the harness alone before changing any application contract.\n\nKeep the repair narrow: one test boundary and its explanation, with normal independent review; no automatic retry of every failed package, new workflow state or blanket exemption for frontier errors. Historical cross-process custody repairs dealt with real witness and actor-map safety and should stay closed. The current head-wait task concerns a measurement count and is separate. I searched the available full request history through the source history captured during this sweep and found repeated review disclosures but no dedicated assignment for this test isolation; this is a recommendation for your review, not an adopted implementation plan. If accepted, file one bounded follow-up rather than continuing to repay the same retry cost in unrelated tasks.","body":{"head":"32660749aad93bff40c93f6aedf6b80a186dd489"}}